<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  NativePHP scaffolds this file with the three JIT/memory entitlements Electron
  and the PHP runtime need, and nothing else. For most apps that is right.

  For this one it is the difference between working and silently not.

  The hardened runtime checks com.apple.security.device.audio-input *before* TCC
  is consulted. Without it, a signed app cannot record and cannot ask to: no
  prompt appears, and the app never shows up in System Settings > Privacy &
  Security > Microphone at all. An empty list there is not "denied", it is "the
  request never happened" — and it presents exactly like a healthy pipeline that
  simply hears nothing. That failure cost the Swift build a long session; see
  docs/ENGINES.md.

  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is necessary but not sufficient, and NativePHP
  already sets it in electron-builder.mjs under mac.extendInfo. Both gates have
  to be open, and the failure with only one of them looks identical to the
  failure with neither.

  This file is also electron-builder's `entitlementsInherit`, which is what gets
  applied to the nested Mach-O binaries inside the bundle — including
  extras/sonocles-cli, the process that actually opens the microphone. That is
  why the entitlement belongs here rather than only on the outer app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
